Availability - 
	Availability measures how often your system is operational and accessible to users. A highly available system continues functioning even when individual components fail.
	Availability = Uptime / (Uptime + Downtime)
	The "Nines" of Availability - one - 90% , two - 99%, three - 99.9% , four - 99.99%, five - 99.999% , six - 99.9999%
	
	
	
Availability in serires vs parallel - 
	Components in series - 
		When components are in series, meaning all must work for the system to function, availability multiplies: if web server is 99.9% , app server is 99.9% and db server is 99.9% then overall is  - 99.7%
		
	Components in parallel - 
		When components are in parallel, meaning any can handle the request, availability improves dramatically: two servers after a load balancer with failure - 0.01% then it makes both of them failure rate - 0.0001%
		
		
		
Possible failures - 
	Hardware failures - 
		hard drive , ssd, server, network switch , power supply.
	software failures - 
		bugs, memory leaks, deadlocks.
	Network failures - 
		Packet loss, Latency spikes, Partition, DNS failures.
	Human failures - 
		Configuration mistake, Failed deployment, Accidental deletions.
		
		
		
Redundancy is the foundation of availability -
	Redundancy is the foundational technique behind almost every availability strategy. Having more than one of something means that any single failure leaves at least one working component still able to serve traffic.
	1.) Active-Passive (Standby) - one component handles all the work while another waits idle as a backup. When the active component fails, the passive one takes over.
		the standby can be configured in different states of readiness -> cold standby , warm standby and hot standby
		1.) Cold standby - The backup server is not running, so failover requires booting the machine, starting services, and potentially restoring data. This might take 5-15 minutes
		2.) warm standby - keeps the backup running and configured, but not actively processing requests. It might be receiving replicated data but is not in the load balancer pool.
		3.) Hot standby - most expensive but fastest.The backup is fully synchronized and ready to serve immediately.
	2.) Active-Active - all components handle traffic simultaneously. There is no distinction between primary and backup because every node is doing real work.
	3.) Geographic Redundancy - what if the entire data center goes offline? Geographic redundancy distributes your system across multiple physical locations:
		cloud providers offer different levels of geographic redundancy - 
			Availability zones, Regions, Multi-cloud
			1.) Availabity zones - seperate data centers in the same region, protects from single data center failure
			2.) Regions - seperate areas like us-east and us-west , protects from regional disasters.
			3.) Multi cloud - different cloud providers (gcp and aws)
	4.) Redundancy across Layers -  True high availability requires redundancy at every layer of your stack.
	
	Redundancy is not free. Every backup server, every replica, every additional availability zone costs money. The question is whether that cost is justified by the reduction in downtime risk



High Availability Patterns - 
	Pattern 1: Load Balancer with Multiple Backends - 
		The load balancer itself is a single point of failure. For true high availability, you need redundant load balancers
	Pattern 2: Database Replication with Automatic Failover - 
		Databases are stateful and cannot simply be load-balanced like web servers. Database high availability requires replication and careful failover management.
		synchronus replication confirms the write after the replica acknowledges (zero data loss) and asynchronous confirms it immediately (possible data loss)
	Pattern 3: Queue-Based Load Leveling - 
		When downstream services cannot handle peak load, use a queue to buffer requests and process them at a sustainable rate. 
		how it provides high availability - Decouples producers from consumers, Buffers traffic spikes that would overwhelm the database, Workers can fail and restart without losing messages, Can scale workers independently based on queue depth
	Pattern 4: Circuit Breaker - 
		When a dependency fails, continuing to call it wastes resources and can cause cascading failures. The circuit breaker pattern prevents this by failing fast.
		A circuit breaker is a pattern used in distributed systems to prevent one failing service from bringing down everything else.
		there are three states :
			1.) closed - this is normal state. The circuit breaker watches the requests and keeps track of failures.If the configured threshold is exceeded, the circuit breaker opens.
			2.) Open - This is the important part. When the circuit is OPEN, requests are not sent to the Payment Service at all. This saves the services.
			3.) HALF-OPEN - Confusing part . After some time when the circuit thinks that the service is healthy again , it moves to half-open and allows some of the requests, if requests are success then move to closed othewise open.
			
