Reliability - 
	Reliability is the probability that a system will perform its intended function correctly over a given period of time, under specified conditions.
	
This definition has several important parts:
	"Correctly" means producing the right output, not just any output.
	"Over a given period" means reliability is measured over time, not at a single instant.
	"Under specified conditions" means we define what normal operation looks like.
	
Measuring Reliability - 
	1. Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF) - measures the average time between failures. A higher MTBF means failures are less frequent. MTBF = Total Operating Time / Number of Failures
		if a system ran for 10,000 hours and experienced 5 failures, MTBF = 10,000 / 5 = 2,000 hours.
	2. Mean Time To Recovery (MTTR) - 
		MTTR measures how long it takes to restore the system after a failure. A lower MTTR means faster recovery.
		MTTR = Total Downtime / Number of Failures
		if 5 failures occurred and total repair time was 10 hours, MTTR = 10 / 5 = 2 hours per failure.
	3. Error Rate
		Percentage of requests that result in errors.
		Error Rate = Failed Requests / Total Requests × 100%
	4. Data Correctness
		Percentage of responses that contain correct data.
		Correctness = Correct Responses / Total Responses × 100%
		
Why Systems Become Unreliable - 
	Hardware Failures
		Physical components wear out and fail over time. Disks develop bad sectors, memory cells corrupt, CPUs overheat, and network cards malfunction.
	Software Bugs 
		every request that hits the buggy code path fails in the same way.The most dangerous bugs are those that do not crash the system but silently produce wrong results.
	Configuration Errors
		AWS's S3 outage in 2017 was triggered by a command to remove a small number of servers that accidentally removed a much larger set.
	Human Error
		
	Overload and Cascading Failures
		Systems that work perfectly under normal load can fail catastrophically when overloaded. 
		
Key Principles of Reliable Systems - 
	Redundancy
		having backup components ready to take over if one part fails.
	Failover Mechanisms
		a system automatically switches to a redundant or standby component when a failure is detected.
	Load Balancing
		prevents any single server from becoming a single point of failure.
	Monitoring and Alerting
	
	Graceful Degradation
		Even when parts of the system fail, a well-designed system can still provide core functionality rather than going completely offline. This concept is known as graceful degradation.
		

Techniques to Enhance Reliability - 
	1. Redundant Architectures - if one fails, other continue operating.
	2. Data Replication - ensure your data is not stored in a single location.
	3. Graceful Degradation - When parts of the system fail, graceful degradation keeps the core functionality working.
	4. Circuit Breakers - cutting off requests to prevent overload, allowing the system to recover.
	5. Idempotency - Idempotent operations produce the same result regardless of how many times they are executed.
	
A reliable system doesn't just stay alive. It keeps doing the right thing even when parts of it start misbehaving.

