Single Point Of Failure - 
	A single point of failure is any component, dependency process, or decision point whose failure can take down the system. 
	
The component can be anything - server, load balancer, db, cloud region, DNS provider.

What makes something a spof - whose failure cause a disrespect.



How to identify SPOFs - 
	- map the user flow and identify if this dependency fails, what happens to the flow?
	- Trace the runtime and recovery dependencies - if one dependency fails while the service is up , you can't change it's credentials or redeploy. if the command which can restore the failed dependency is manual and untested and only run by one person only.
	- if two components depend on each other to run then one failure can cause the other component to fail.
	- do the things you assume will fail, fail them yourself and see if the system works well and can recover.



Strategies to Reduce SPOFs - 
	1. Redundancy - it means having other component take over when one fails.
	2. Load Balancing and Health Checks - only if load balancer layer itself is redundant.
	3. Data Replication and Backup - just duplicate the data across databases.
	4. Geographic and Failure-Domain Isolation
	5. Graceful Degradation - if recommendation is not working , at least show the trending items.
	
