Latency vs Throughput vs Bandwidth - 
	
Latency - 
	the time it takes for a single request from source to destination and back.
	
	Components of Latency - 
		Propagation delay (time taken through the medium like optic fibres), Transmition delay (time to push bits onto the wire), Processing delay (time for routers, load balancers and servers to process), Queuing delay (time in buffers when components are busy).
		
	Latency is measured as - p50, p90, p99, p99.1 , it's the percentage and remaining %age users experience terrible performance.


	What affects Latency - Geographical distance, Network congestion, Server load, Database queries, DNS resolution, TLs handshake.

	Reducing Latency - Use CDNs, Caching, Connection pooling, Database optimization, Geographical distribution, Protocol optimization.
	

Throughput - 
	Throughput is the amount of work completed per unit of time. Requests per second. 
	
	Bandwidth is maximum possible data transfer rate and throughput is actual data transfer achieved.
	
	Throughput is - workers /latency.
	Throughput is reduced by - CPU bound tasks, large memory sets, I/O operations, Connection limit, Locks/shared resources
	
	Improving throughput - Horizontal scaling, Vertical scaling, Async processing, Batching, Caching, Connection pooling, Load balancing.
	
Bandwidth - 
	maximum rate at which data can be transferred.
	it's bits per second
	Types of Bandwidth - 
		Network bandwidth - Capacity of network links(1Gbps), 
		Memory bandwidth - Rate of data transfer to/from RAM (DDR4 ~25GB/s)
		Disk bandwidth - Read/write speed of storage (SSD: ~500 MB/s)
		Bus bandwidth - Internal data transfer rate (PCIe 4.0 x16: ~32 GB/s, PCIe 5.0 x16: ~64 GB/s)
	
	Bandwidth-Delay Product (BDP) = Bandwidth × Latency - the amount of data that can be in flight.
	if bandwidth - 100mbps and latency is 50ms then bdp - 500 * 0.05 = 625KB. that means - 625 KB of data can be in transit at once.
	
