Fault Tolerance - 
	a system's ability to continue working even when some part of it fails.
	
1. redundancy = better fault tolerance

2. How to achieve Fault Tolerance - 
	mulitple hardware systems - run same workload on different servers
	multiple software systems - run multiple instances of a server.
	
	The idea is don't let one component become the single point of failure.

3. Fault tolerance vs High Availability
	FT - ability to continue operating when something fails
	HA - Goal of keeping the system available for a very high percentage of time.
	
	A system still needs downtime for software upgrades, database migrations or maintenance so FT helps achieving HA, but doesn't guarantee it.
	
4. Normal Functioning vs Graceful Degradation
	NF - Failure happens -> users don't notice. one server dies another server immediately handles the traffic.
	GD - failure happens -> some functionality becoms slower/unavailable, but the entire application doesn't go down. e.g - recommendations doesn't work but main shopping functionality continues.
	
	NF is better but it costs more.

5. Levels of FT - 
	Node failure , AZ failure, Region failure, Cloud provider failure.
